x86/intel: keep fast strings in a guest

The boot clears REP_GOOD and ERMS when IA32_MISC_ENABLE has no fast
string bit. In a guest the MSR is the value of the VMM (KVM gives 0
for the bit), and the CPU runs the string instructions with the
setting of the host. A guest now keeps REP_GOOD and ERMS, so memcpy,
memset, and the clear of a page use rep movs and rep stos.

Measured on KVM, 1 CPU, 64 MB, root on dm-verity: the start of init
is 0.05 ms (best) to 0.4 ms (mean of 8) earlier.

--- a/arch/x86/kernel/cpu/intel.c
+++ b/arch/x86/kernel/cpu/intel.c
@@ -333,10 +333,15 @@
 	 *
 	 * Adhere to the preference and program the Linux-defined fast
 	 * string flag and enhanced fast string capabilities accordingly.
+	 *
+	 * A guest keeps fast strings: its IA32_MISC_ENABLE is the value
+	 * that the VMM gives, and the CPU runs the string instructions of
+	 * the guest with the setting of the host.
 	 */
 	if (c->x86_vfm >= INTEL_PENTIUM_M_DOTHAN) {
 		rdmsrq(MSR_IA32_MISC_ENABLE, misc_enable);
-		if (misc_enable & MSR_IA32_MISC_ENABLE_FAST_STRING) {
+		if ((misc_enable & MSR_IA32_MISC_ENABLE_FAST_STRING) ||
+		    cpu_has(c, X86_FEATURE_HYPERVISOR)) {
 			/* X86_FEATURE_ERMS is set based on CPUID */
 			set_cpu_cap(c, X86_FEATURE_REP_GOOD);
 		} else {
